      "id": "GENUS_ERINACEUS",
      "name": "Erinaceus",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "True hedgehogs of Europe and Asia — iconic, spiny insectivores that have inhabited the Old World for around 15 million years. Their 5,000–7,000 hollow keratin spines are modified hairs, and when threatened a hedgehog can roll into a near-perfect ball by contracting the orbicularis muscle that runs around its back, protecting all exposed soft tissue. Hedgehogs are partial hibernators, entering torpor when temperatures drop and food becomes scarce. The Western European hedgehog has declined by roughly 50% in the UK since 2000, driven by agricultural intensification, habitat fragmentation, road mortality, and badger predation.",
      "lineage": "Hedgehogs",
